  <text>
    <body>
      <div>
        <head>In the directories</head>
        <p>The Leicester Hotel is in the directories in <ref target="civic?id=Leicester%20Street">Leicester Street</ref> from 1857 to 1915. It is No. 31 from 1859 to 1861, No. 36 from 1862 to 1873, No. 32 in 1879 to 1885, and No. 56 from 1890. From 1867 to 1871 it is listed as the Leicester hotel and stables, and in 1871 as vacant.</p>
        <p>The directories list the proprietors as George Woodward (1857 and 1858), James Woodward (1859 to 1861), John Curtain (1862 to 1866), T. O'Brien (1867 to 1870), C. O'Brien (1872 and 1873), John Ennis (1874 to 1880, printed Ennas in 1874 and 1875), Mrs Catherine Ennis (1885 and 1890), Mrs Ellen Paul (1895), Mrs E. McDowell (1900) and Mrs M. Lancaster (1905 to 1915).</p>
      </div>
      <div>
        <head>Neighbours</head>
        <p>The hotel was among the stables of Leicester Street. F. R. G. Woodward, stable keeper, is next door at No. 28 in 1857 and 1858, Stephen Newington and then John Blackburn kept livery and bait stables at No. 27 (1859 to 1866), and the <ref target="civic?id=Leicester%20Stables">Leicester Stables</ref> are listed beside the hotel from 1859 to 1862. R. T. Whelan, farrier and veterinary surgeon, is next door from 1869 to 1872. <ref target="civic?id=James%20C.%20Steel">James C. Steel</ref>, hide merchant and tanner, is at No. 34 from 1872 to 1880, and James Edmonds, baker, at No. 30 from 1874 to 1885. Later neighbours are W. H. Rocke &amp; Co.'s cabinet factory, later zinc ornamental workers, at No. 58 (1890 to 1905), Sargents Ltd, pastrycooks, at No. 52 (1910 and 1915), and the <ref target="civic?id=Buckley%20%26%20Nunn%20stables">Buckley &amp; Nunn stables</ref> at No. 58 in 1915.</p>
      </div>
